// A capture plan (`dev_capture::plan::CapturePlan`), the game's damage-model view (game state
// only): the kill cam's lesson, the player's own tank knocked out and shown in the glass look over
// the live world, its components tinted and the record's fields drawn as one volume
// (`pen_cam_look.wgsl`, reading the fields' depth pictures that `pen_cam_picture.wgsl` renders),
// with the record's elements (`pen_cam_glow.wgsl`) and the struck components' outlines
// (`pen_cam_outline.wgsl`). The offline target scenario `OVERMATCH_TARGET=kills` parks a target
// that fires into the player's tank from 25 s, every 10 s, until the knockout (`tank::scenario`;
// its trigger runs on `Time<Real>`, so this plan fixes no frame step and its frames land a little
// differently run to run). The frame times assume the first round knocks the tank out (ASSUMED);
// a later knockout moves the lesson past them. From the checkout root, hidden and silent under the
// machine's build lock:
//
//   /usr/bin/lockf -k -t 10800 /tmp/overmatch-cargo.lock env OVERMATCH_TARGET=kills \
//     OVERMATCH_UNATTENDED=1 OVERMATCH_WINDOW=hidden \
//     OVERMATCH_CAPTURE=scripts/capture/pen-cam-killcam.ron target/debug/overmatch --offline
//
// writes target/capture/pen-cam-killcam-<second>.png at each frame second, then exits. Times and
// size are capture configuration (ASSUMED), not game data.
(
    frames: (
        out: "target/capture/pen-cam-killcam.png",
        at: [26.5, 28.0, 29.5],
        size: (1280, 720),
        scale_factor: 1.0,
        ui: false,
        gizmos: false,
    ),
)
